  "query_preview": "..(After midnight Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 614 AM EDT WED OCT 28 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, 500 mb ridging will be \ncentered over the southeastern US, with a deep closed low over the\nPacific Northwest. Meanwhile, a shortwave trough will initially \nbe located over far western Texas, translating northeastward with \ntime. At the surface, an associated area of low pressure will be \nlocated near the Oklahoma/New Mexico border, with a surface high \njust southeast of eastern Kentucky. As the initial shortwave \ntranslates northeast, this low will lift northeastward as well, \ndragging a frontal boundary northward with it. In eastern \nKentucky, a preexisting boundary will dissipate with time, being \ndisplaced from upper level support and any low pressure center; \nthus, expect dry weather to continue through the end of the \nworkweek under the influence of the midlevel ridge, with high \ntemperatures rising into the mid to upper 70s, above average for \nthis time of year. Overnight, cloud cover associated with the \napproaching shortwave will build, keeping lows warm in the upper \n40s and low 50s. Cloud cover should linger through the day on \nFriday, suppressing temperatures. Highs will \"only\" rise into the \nlow to mid 70s. \n\nAlthough the weather has been dry, Saturday night will bring the \nfirst mentionable PoPs in over two weeks. A second shortwave will \napproach the area, although there are still notable differences in\ntiming and impacts among deterministic guidance. The ECMWF favors\ndry weather through the weekend, although this seems to be an \noutlier solution at this time, with LREF probabilities for \nmeasurable precipitation this weekend generally in the 50-60% \nrange for our southwestern counties, with these chances decreasing\ncloser to the West Virginia border. Regardless, atmospheric \nmoisture will be anomalously high at this time, with LREF mean PW \nvalues in the 0.8-1.0 inch range across the forecast area on \nSunday morning, roughly in the 75th percentile or higher compared \nto climatology for this time of year. With these ingredients in \nplace, those looking to enjoy festivities on Halloween night are \nencouraged to monitor future forecast changes as the timing and \nimpact of this shortwave become clearer. \n\nMore substantial rain chances will arrive next week as the \nremnants of Tropical Storm Vance drift northeast, interacting with\nthe upper level low initially located over the Pacific Northwest,\nalthough this far out, details are still fuzzy due to increasing \nmodel spread. At the surface, a well defined area of low pressure \nshould also push eastward toward the forecast area. Rain chances \nwill increase in the vicinity of this low's associated warm \nfront, expected to lift north through eastern Kentucky. This \nsystem will interact with the preexisting moist air mass,\nincreasing confidence in rainfall; however, with poor model \nconsistency at this stage, only scattered PoPs are in place early \nnext week, more a function of low confidence in the evolution of \nthis complicated pattern than a low chance for rain.",
